## Not letters, not words

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 reads text in chunks called <GlossaryPopover term="token">tokens</GlossaryPopover>. A
token is often a whole word, and sometimes a piece of one. Each token has a number. Type below to
see how GPT-2 cuts up your text.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2 doesn't read letters one at a time, and it doesn't read whole words either. It reads <GlossaryPopover term="token">tokens</GlossaryPopover>: chunks of text that are often a whole common
word, sometimes part of a rarer one, and sometimes a single character. Each token has an ID number.
Type below to see how GPT-2 splits your text.

</At>

<LiveTokenizer />

<At levels="simple">

Common words are usually one token, and the space before a word usually belongs to it. Rarer
words are cut into pieces you can often recognize, like *token* + *ization*. GPT-2 has a fixed
list of tokens, and every text is made from them.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A few things to notice:

- Most common words are one token, and the space before a word is usually part of the token:
  *" cat"* (with the space) is token 3797.
- Longer or rarer words are split into pieces you can often recognize, like *token* + *ization*.
- GPT-2 knows exactly 50,257 different tokens. Every possible text is made of them.<Cite id="radford2019" />

</At>

## How the pieces are chosen

<At levels="simple">

Why *token* + *ization* and not *tok* + *enization*? The pieces are found automatically, from lots
of text. Start with single letters. Find the two neighbors that appear together most often, and
glue them into one new piece. Repeat. This is called **byte pair encoding**. Step through it below
on a tiny example.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Why *token* + *ization* and not *tok* + *enization*? The pieces are learned from text by a simple
method called **byte pair encoding** (BPE), first used for language models by Sennrich and
colleagues in 2016.<Cite id="sennrich2016" /> Start with single characters, then repeatedly find
the pair of neighbors that appears most often and glue it into a new token.

Step through it below on the tiny example from their paper. Each merge adds one token to the
vocabulary, and the text needs fewer tokens to write down.

</At>

<BPEStepper />

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 did the same with lots of web text. Frequent words ended up as one token; rare words are
built from a few.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2 ran the same process on bytes (the numbers from the last chapter) over a large amount of web
text, and kept merging until it had 50,257 tokens.<Cite id="radford2019" /> Frequent words end up as
one token; rare words are built from a few.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### GPT-2's vocabulary, counted

GPT-2's tokens are this process written out. It starts from the 256 possible values of a byte, so
any text at all, in any language or with emoji, can be written down even where no merge
applies.<Cite id="radford2019" /> On top of those it learned 50,000 merges, and it adds one special
token, `<|endoftext|>` (number 50,256), that marks the end of a text:<Cite id="gpt2-config" />

$$
|V| = 256 + 50{,}000 + 1 = 50{,}257
$$

where $|V|$ is the number of tokens in the vocabulary. Each merge picks the pair of neighboring
symbols that occurs most often in the training text:

$$
(a, b) = \operatorname*{arg\,max}_{(x,\,y)} \operatorname{count}(x\,y)
$$

where $x\,y$ is any symbol $x$ followed directly by a symbol $y$, $\operatorname{count}(x\,y)$ is how
many times that happens, and the winning pair $(a, b)$ becomes the new token $ab$. GPT-2's first
merge was *" t"*: a space followed by *t*.

The real tokenizer has rules the toy doesn't. GPT-2 never merges across kinds of characters, such
as letters and punctuation, except that a space can join what follows it. Without that rule, common
words would use up tokens on variants like *dog.*, *dog!*, and *dog?*.<Cite id="radford2019" /> And
to split new text, the tokenizer doesn't count anything: it applies the merges it learned, earliest
first.<Cite id="sennrich2016" />

</At>

## Why "strawberry" is tricky

<At levels="simple">

Try *strawberry* in the tokenizer above, with and without a space in front. With a space, as it
usually appears in a sentence, it's a single token. The model never sees the letters inside it, so
when asked how many *r*s are in strawberry, it can't just look and count.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Try typing *strawberry* in the tokenizer above, with and without a space in front.

Without a space, GPT-2 splits it into *st* + *raw* + *berry*. With a space, as it usually appears in
a sentence, *" strawberry"* is a single token, number 41236. The model never sees the letters inside
it. Asked how many *r*s are in strawberry, the model can only answer from what it has learned about
token 41236, not by looking at the letters.

</At>

## Every model has its own alphabet

<At levels="simple">

Tick *GPT-4* and *GPT-4o* in the tokenizer to compare. Newer models learned bigger lists of
tokens, so the same text usually takes fewer of them. A token number only makes sense to the model
it belongs to.

The difference is biggest for code. GPT-2 uses a token for nearly every space at the start of a
line, so a short program can take almost twice as many GPT-2 tokens as GPT-4 tokens. Even one
company's models can count differently: Anthropic says its newer Claude models use about 30% more
tokens for the same text.<Cite id="anthropic-pricing" /> So a price per token only tells you so
much; count your own text.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Tick *GPT-4* and *GPT-4o* in the tokenizer to compare. Newer models learned bigger vocabularies
(called `cl100k_base` and `o200k_base`), so the same text usually takes fewer
tokens.<Cite id="tiktoken" /> A token ID only makes sense to the model that uses that tokenizer.

The difference is biggest for code. Paste this five-line function into the tokenizer:

```python
def count_words(text):
    words = text.split()
    if not words:
        return 0
    return len(words)
```

It takes 44 GPT-2 tokens but 26 with GPT-4's tokenizer, while the sentence *The cat sat on the
mat. It looked out of the window and watched the birds fly by.* takes 20 in all three. GPT-2 spends
a token on nearly every space of indentation, so `        return 0` alone is 9 tokens; GPT-4's
tokenizer has tokens for runs of spaces and needs 4.
Even one company's models can count differently: Anthropic says its Claude 4.7 and later models
produce about 30% more tokens for the same text than earlier ones.<Cite id="anthropic-pricing" />
So a price per token only compares models that share a tokenizer. To compare costs, count your own
text.

</At>

## Counting in tokens

<At levels="simple">

How much a model can read at once, and what it costs to use, are counted in tokens, not words. The
six nursery rhymes from <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" /> are 145 words but 195 GPT-2
tokens. Other languages often need many more tokens for the same text.<Cite id="petrov2023" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

How much a model can read at once, and what it costs to use, are counted in tokens, not words. The
six nursery rhymes from <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" /> are 145 words and 195 GPT-2
tokens, about 1.3 tokens per word: punctuation marks are tokens of their own, and rarer words take
more than one. Other
languages often need many more tokens for the same meaning. In one study, the same text took up to
15 times as many tokens in some languages as in others.<Cite id="petrov2023" /> That leaves less
room in the model's window and costs more.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Models read tokens: chunks of text between single characters and whole words, each with an ID.
- Byte pair encoding builds the vocabulary by repeatedly merging the most common pair.
- Frequent words become one token; rare words are split into pieces. The model doesn't see letters
  inside a token.
- Limits and prices are counted in tokens, and other languages often need more of them.

<At levels="deep">

- GPT-2's 50,257 tokens are 256 bytes, 50,000 merges, and one end-of-text token.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** a token ID like 3797 is just a label. How does the model know that *cat* is more
like *kitten* than like *carpet*?
